#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""zbench: one tool that measures Zinc on every machine, with one corpus, one result format and one comparison.

usage:
  tools/zbench run  DEVICE [--suite render|media|boundary] [--scene id,id] [--runs 3] [--frames 240] [--skip 30] [--mode uncapped|paced|both] [--backend auto,cpu,gl,glfinish]
                    [--size 1024x768] [--no-build] [--app-id ID]
  tools/zbench show FILE [FILE ...]          one table: a column per file, a row per scene, ratios to the first file
  tools/zbench gate BASE.json NEW.json [--tolerance 0.15]    exit 3 when a scene got slower than the noise allows, 4 on a rendering problem
  tools/zbench list                          the scenes of the render suite

DEVICE: mac | android | webos | ios   (one device on adb / novacom / usb; ios needs --app-id, see below)

What it does, for the render suite: builds tests/bench/render_suite ONCE for the device (one app, not one app per scene), installs it, then launches it once per
scene and per run (ZB_SCENE=<id>), interleaving the runs (scene 1 run 1, scene 2 run 1, ..., scene 1 run 2) so that a thermal drift hits every scene alike.
The engine's own profiler measures the frames (ZINC_PROFILE=json: p50/p99/max/mean per phase, the interval between frames, late-frame counts, the GPU's
strings) after a warm-up (ZINC_PROFILE_SKIP); ZINC_FRAMEHASH=last records the hash of the last frame. The machine's state (model, OS, CPU and GPU, governor,
frequencies, temperatures, battery, load) is recorded before and after. One JSON artifact per run of the tool goes to docs/reports/bench/zbench-*.json.

Reading the numbers (docs/reports/render-bench-research.md): the median of the per-run medians is the figure; `spread` is the run-to-run range over the median and is
the noise floor of that scene on that machine; a mean is never reported. `uncapped` asks for no vsync (ZINC_VSYNC=0, honoured by the SDL GL window HAL only):
the frame interval is then the cost of a frame; `paced` keeps vsync and answers "does it hold the refresh rate". `vsync_locked` says which one the machine gave.
Problems are recorded, not hidden: a frame hash that changes between runs of one scene (non-deterministic drawing or a driver defect), fewer frames than asked,
a paced run with late frames, a spread above 10 %.

--backend picks the renderer (ZINC_RENDERER): auto (the device's default), cpu (the software raster), gl (the GPU replay: OpenGL / OpenGL ES 2), glfinish (gl with the glFinish
timer, ZINC_GL_TIMER=finish, for a GPU that has no timer query: gl_timer_source says which source a number came from; never compare numbers of different sources). Several backends
run side by side in one artifact (modes are keyed `uncapped`, `uncapped/gl`...).

ios: builds with the bundle id of an app of yours already on the device (ZINC_IOS_TEAM, ZINC_PYMD3, ZINC_IOS_UDID; that app is REPLACED, its data kept; no contact with
Apple: the Xcode-managed profile already on this Mac is used), docs/reports/ipad-16-bringup.md.
"""
import datetime, glob, json, os, platform, re, shlex, shutil, signal, statistics, subprocess, sys, time

